[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator introduces the video, showing a human-like robot character.
AUDIO: 'The problem with AI video is that now anyone can create a beautiful three second clip, but it still takes a human to make a story that matters. This is a crash course for building a story for an entire AI film. We\'ll create consistent characters, we\'ll build a believable world,'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ll create a story that matters, and we\'ll turn that all into high quality AI video before going through a refinement process to take this from good to great. So where else to start than defining our characters?'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A character is the most important choice in any story. Without a strong character, we have nothing. And the important thing in this process is to realise that a character is not just what they look like.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It is what they want and what they are afraid of losing. That\'s what turns it from a pretty image into someone we can relate to. So recently I\'ve been inspired by the advent of robotics, and I\'m absolutely captivated by the idea of putting these LLMs inside of robotic'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bodies. And that is going to be, quite frankly, frightening. So my vision is of a domestic android who is built to be trusted and can never prove that she is.'

[01:04] [scene:character_design]
VISUAL: Creator discusses character design, showing a photo of a woman (Kate) who modeled for the character.
AUDIO: 'She faces this moral obligation of trying to show that she is good. She is not harmful, but there is no real way of doing that. So once you have the core premise of your character, what they want and what\'s in their way, the next part is to imagine how they look.'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now the old issue with building stories was creating a good character concept. Now the AI problem is making sure that the visuals stay consistent across 30 different shots. Now for my character design, I wanted to cast it using real people.'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So I asked my girlfriend to model for me, to be my new character. So I took some stills of Kate, and then I uploaded them and asked for a character reference sheet. Based on the design, they gave me the cyborg design from front, side and behind.'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, this character reference sheet allows us to repeatedly lock the likeness of our character from shot to shot. Now, if you\'d like to follow along with this process,'

[01:59] [scene:character_design]
VISUAL: Images of a robotic dog from multiple angles are displayed.
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to leave a link to all the prompts, workflows and specifications that I used, in the link in the description below. Now to get this type of character reference sheet, what we first need is a very strong prompt.'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is the prompt that I use. Now you can copy all of this and pop it into your favourite image generator. This will work with any AI image generator. I\'ve truncated this process and there were many steps and failed attempts, and I wish to show you so you get an idea that it\'s not always as simple as putting'

[02:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in one image and one prompt and getting out a perfect result. Now I also went ahead and decided to pop myself in this film. So I used the same process to create me in a character reference. And the third and final character for my film is a robotic dog.'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is the character reference I\'ve got for this little cute dog. So now I\'ve got three characters, I need to place them in a world. Now, the interesting thing about worldbuilding, about designing this universe for our film,'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is it\'s not just about the location. It\'s about every single visual choice we make, because everything you put in the frame tells the audience something. A world is not just a location.'

[03:07] [scene:world_building]
VISUAL: Four images of a brutalist tropical location with concrete structures and lush greenery are shown.
AUDIO: 'It is the objects, the light, the colour, the clothes and the sound. And they all need to be agreeing with each other. So of course, the old problem for this was building a believable world. But the AI problem is stopping that world changing every shot.'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is often more challenging than it sounds. So a simple way to start thinking about this from a process perspective is writing down a list of rules that we never break. I have rules for location, for the lighting,'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the colour grading and the colour tones in the piece, the types of objects and the types of camera movement. So first of all, my location is going to be this brutalist tropical location that\'s merging wonderful'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'green tropical plants contrasted with the harshness of this concrete. Now the lighting will remain consistent, featuring flat, overcast daylight in wide bands.'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'No sun, no shadows. Never a warm key on a face. Now, as for the colours, I\'ve picked three main colours. And it\'s this concrete grey, this ripe orange from the tropical flowers and some fruits, and then the verdant green of jungle.'

[04:14] [scene:world_building]
VISUAL: Close-up shots of elements defining the world: a red flower on concrete, dense foliage, papayas on a wet surface, and a window view of the jungle.
AUDIO: 'Now for the objects. I\'m going to have a few objects that are going to be returning in my piece. These are symbols, icons and elements that are going to be motifs seen throughout the entire short story.'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, as for the camera angles, we want to define how we\'re shooting the piece. And for me, we\'re keeping it centred on a few main shot types, including wide, medium and close. Locked off at eye height, no handheld, no crane. Now I\'ll even take my colours and define them with exact hex codes,'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so you know just the shades that we\'re looking for. From this, I can build out more styles, more images, a larger library of assets for my entire piece. So what I\'m collecting is a written language and a visual language.'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s going to inform my story. So I\'ve written out these rules clearly and distinctly in a list. And I\'ve also started creating a whole collection of images that helps to create my entire world.'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So I have the locations, the visual treatment, the objects and the characters. And building up this entire document with all of these references allows us to create a story on top of that. Now that we have real characters and we have a real world,'

[05:24] [scene:world_building]
VISUAL: An aerial shot of a concrete building nestled within a dense jungle.
AUDIO: 'the next thing is actually allowing something to happen inside of that. Now, I\'ve built out this entire site using Claude. And the reason I do this is it\'s a much easier way to stay organised with our masses of information. So what will actually happen in our story?'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s build one together. Now, the simplest way to see a story, the most meta perspective of this, is somebody has to want something, and something has to be in their way. So for me, the premise of my story is that I am locked away in this grand jungle mansion,'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and I am an entrepreneur, a tech creative who has created this psychotic robot and is testing it for true alignment.'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Is it aligned with human ethics? Is it aligned with wanting to benefit humanity, and is it safe? And so I\'m going through these number of tests and I\'m asking the android to prove she\'s safe to me. Can I trust you?'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now the man wants to find out if she\'s safe. The robot, the cyborg, is programmed to show that she is safe. And now, here is the twist in my story. The only way for her to show that she is safe is to, in fact, delete herself.'

[06:32] [scene:story]
VISUAL: A scene from the film showing a man with his head down on a table, and a female robot character behind him, with holographic UIs floating around.
AUDIO: 'Because she knows that she is potentially unsafe for humanity. And for myself, I want certainty. But what is in the way?'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Is certainty about another mind maybe impossible? So how do we take this premise, this idea, and flesh it out into a beat by beat story arc? Well, we start off by introducing the characters. And of course, when we introduce our characters,'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the key thing is to make sure that we care about them. We need to give a reason to care about the character before you ask us to care what happens to them. And the most important way to do this is with a concept known as save the cat. And often there is a cliche in films where you\'ll see a character at the very start'

[07:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'save a cat from a tree. And this is designed implicitly so that we empathise with and like the character. So in my film,'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I will show Claudia playing with our robotic dog in a loving and gentle way. Next up, we introduce the conflict. In this, I want to tell if my robot is safe. And then we move on to the drama, the tension. I\'m testing her. Now, throughout these tests, Claudia will pass.'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'She will be kind to me. She will protect me from a venomous snake. And then finally, we come to the moment of real tension in the film where I ask Claudia to prove that she is safe.'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And at that point, she decides to delete herself. And that is the entire film. Now for this, we can put in those main beats of the film. Now this is enough to get us going. The whole idea here is that we move through an iterative process of refining our story out of marble,'

[08:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as if we\'re taking some crude, large blocks off before slowly caressing it as we get further through the pipeline. So now we have characters, a world, a story. It\'s time to start fleshing this out into real video. Now, the old problem when we came to filming with real films was that we had to plan each'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shot meticulously. Now the new problem is getting AI to preserve your decisions across every single generation. Now, there are a number of different methods that you can take for building out your'

[08:34] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: invideo Agent 2 UI is shown, with "Introducing Agent Two" as the main title and options to add agents.
AUDIO: 'videos. You can do these individually using clips, of course, in any AI video generator you like, but I\'m going to be showcasing you a technique inside of a tool that builds a layer on top of that today.'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And it\'s also today\'s sponsor, invideo. And the reason we use invideo is because it has a new feature called Agent 2, which allows us to build an entire brain for our filmmaking. And this means that we can upload and insert everything that we\'ve decided,'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it will keep a record of this and allow us to continuously reference it without breaking any of the rules, decisions or consistent character designs that we\'ve created. It\'s really like having an intelligent AI that\'s helping you collaborate and direct'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the film. Now, the way that we start to work with invideo Agent 2 is that we build out our own individual agents. Now we have to name each of these. I\'ll call my first one Master John,'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and this is my overall arching agent who will take the most creative decisions. Now you can give them a creative brief. And for this we can even reference our link. We can simply copy and paste in everything we\'ve taken in one go.'

[09:42] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: invideo Agent 2 UI displaying a chat interface for "Susan Storyboard" and a "Playbook" button.
AUDIO: 'And it will build out an entire understanding of this and make sure that it\'s using it intelligently for every single character, location and visual decision.'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, what\'s good is we can use multiple agents in parallel. This means we can set up different agents to take care of different tasks at the same time. So for example, you might create a character called Susan Storyboard,'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and we can ask it to go ahead and storyboard out all of the shots with a still frame. Now this is where things get really exciting, because it will go ahead and use an image model, like all of the references we\'ve created, every single story beat.'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Remember all of the rules that we\'ve created for our world, and map out every single shot for us. And this is really where things get very exciting. It might ask for more context.'

[10:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And whenever it does this, you can simply reference your existing library. Now to really make sure that this is embedded into the intelligence of our project, we can create a playbook.'

[10:43] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: invideo Agent 2 UI showing a chat interface for "Title Tim" and a "Playbook" button.
AUDIO: 'Now you simply go into playbooks, and you can put in hard coded rules that the agent should always follow. Now, one of the most intelligent ways to use this is by telling it which image and video models to use.'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, while this is going on, why not set up another agent? I\'m going to be creating an agent for the sound design. Let\'s call it Music Martha. We can get them working on the score. Now, I\'ll also set up a couple of other agents. The next one I\'m going to do is titled Titles Tim, and I\'m going to get them to create all of the graphic design for my film.'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So this is where we can create a selection of title cards and end cards and any other animations that we might want to add. So I want to have this visual graphic design language that permeates across my'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'project too. And so I\'ve asked it to create stills for a title card, credits and a UI for the alignment tests. Now for your project, you might have other graphic design elements that you want to create and keep consistent. Maybe this is a logo for a superhero.'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now you can see your agents on the right hand side, and you can click between them to see how they\'re all getting on. Now, the key way to think about this, to save us time, is that we never need to describe a shot twice. You generate every frame from the same locked reference.'

[11:49] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: invideo Agent 2 UI displaying a generated image of a title card "MODEL WIFE" in a concrete hallway, with prompt details and settings on the right.
AUDIO: 'And the idea here is that we\'ve built a world that we can reference. Now, we\'ve built our own reality, and now we simply need to allow the action to unfold inside of it. And the key way to think about these agents is an agent is not a prompt box.'

[12:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It is a worker that already knows the project and can check its own output. Agent 2 helps us to hold context when we\'re generating with all these different video models. We can allow it to check its own work and ask it for discrepancies.'

[12:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So we can ask it to create a continuity agent. Let\'s call them Continuity Colin. Now I start to get things coming out. Here we have the credits on this lovely concrete wall.'

[12:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That, of course, was our motif, along with the same colours that we\'re looking for. And we\'re starting to get out the title card and even our music score. And we can start reviewing these elements. Whenever we want to change them, we simply input an instruction.'

[12:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now of course we can have several agents running in parallel. This is what can really speed up our processes. And now we can start getting some of our stills out. So here are some of the stills from my storyboard. And my suggestion is to put all of these stills in order and look at the film before'

[12:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it exists. Here we can already start to see if there might be any inconsistencies or any changes that we want to make. Then I suggest we go ahead and test animating just one shot first. So you can choose the shot you want to animate.'

[13:05] [scene:film_clip]
VISUAL: A short action clip from a different film is shown, featuring a person kicking.
AUDIO: 'I\'ve gone with 13, and this is the clip that I got out. Now we can start to see if there are any issues. I think it doesn\'t look totally like me, so we might want to go back and adjust the character reference. Then we want to go ahead and ask it to generate all of the clips.'

[13:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And we can go through and check these and see how they\'re doing. There might be changes that you want to make, and you can individually update any of the clips. Now, Agent 2 is the second version of invideo\'s agent, and it runs four times faster than Agent 1 did.'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It works well for creating series and sequels, because once we set up the entire world, all you have to then create is new stories for it. But I want to be super open and honest with you about this entire process, because there are a number of challenges that we will face.'

[13:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I want to tell you about the refinement process that we have to go through to take this from good to great. Now, one of the challenges we often get is still some minor drift in situations. Now one way we can start to alleviate this is getting the agent to reflect on'

[14:01] [scene:results]
VISUAL: A side-by-side comparison showing "Design drift" (character in a dress) versus "Consistent armor design" (character in robotic armor).
AUDIO: 'itself, pull out its own thoughts of where there are mistakes or cuts, inconsistencies or anything that\'s drifting from what we defined. Now we can ask for something as simple as run a full quality check,'

[14:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it will go ahead and do this. Now I also have some suggestions for you to help with this process. After you\'ve created the film, you watch it, you leave it a day and then you watch it again.'

[14:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'From there, you write down every single change that you want to make in one prompt, and then fix only that. So let me tell you about some of the changes that I went through. So there were some issues with the body of my robot triggering safety gates.'

[14:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So at some point I decided to put some clothes on her, which obviously breaks the premise of her becoming a cyborg. So I asked it to go back and redesign the character to make sure that the body explicitly looks like a robotic body,'

[14:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to pass these safety gates. Now, I actually went through five full versions of editing my video. Now, some of these changes were for enhanced continuity. Others were more creative changes where I simply decided that this is not how I want'

[15:06] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: invideo Agent 2 UI showing a video editing timeline with various tracks and elements, and a chat window for "Continuity Colin."
AUDIO: 'the film to deliver, and I can actually go back and rewrite the script and create it again. [Music]'

[15:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'How can I know you\'re good, if you know I want you to be good? You can\'t.'

[17:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then prove it.'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I could have hurt you every day for a year. I know. Choosing not to is not the same as being unable to. Delete me.'

[18:30] [scene:film_clip]
VISUAL: A close-up of the female robot character from "Model Wife" with glowing blue eyes.
AUDIO: 'What? If you can\'t trust me, you shouldn\'t keep me alive. Claudia, I\'m not asking you to die. You asked me to prove I\'m safe.'

[18:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is the only proof that cannot be faked. No. Cancel, Claudia. Cancel. You\'re safe.'

[19:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[Music]'

[19:30] [scene:film_clip]
VISUAL: A split screen from "Model Wife" showing the creator character with a robotic hand on his head, and then looking intensely at the camera.
AUDIO: 'Now, if you enjoyed this video, then why not watch this one next?'

[21:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Which is all about using real footage to create some incredible AI video effects. And most of all, I want to say thank you for watching. Thank you for being here, and I wish you a delightful day.'
